--TEST--
Core\IO::lines names the boundaries of what a line is — an empty file has none, a lone terminator is one empty line, and a file that ends without a terminator still ends its last line — and the value it answers may be walked more than once, because `iterate()` opens a fresh walk rather than resuming the one before it — spec § 14, `rule:iteration/two-interfaces`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The first half is the bound asserted on both sides: an empty file and the
// smallest file that has a line in it are one byte apart, and a member that
// counted terminators rather than pieces answers one too many for every body
// here. The lines are printed bracketed rather than counted, so a member that
// left a `\r` glued to a line fails as well.
array<string> $bodies = [
    "",
    "\n",
    "one",
    "one\n",
    "one\n\ntwo",
];
int $total = Core\Arr::count($bodies) as int;

int $i = 0;
for (; $i < $total; $i = $i + 1) {
    Core\IO::write("subject.txt", $bodies[$i]);
    string $shown = "";
    foreach (Core\IO::lines("subject.txt") as string $line) {
        $shown = $shown . "[" . $line . "]";
    }
    echo "body ", $i as string, ": ", $shown, "\n";
}

// The second half is the contract the type carries: `Iterable<string>` is a
// value a `foreach` asks for a walk, not a walk that has already begun. Two
// loops over one value have to see the same file — a value that were the walk
// itself would answer nothing the second time, which is exactly the mistake
// PHP's own generators make available.
Core\IO::write("roster.txt", "one\ntwo\nthree\n");
Core\IO\Lines $held = Core\IO::lines("roster.txt");

int $first = 0;
foreach ($held as string $line) {
    $first = $first + 1;
}

int $second = 0;
foreach ($held as string $line) {
    $second = $second + 1;
}

echo "first walk: ", $first as string, "\n";
echo "second walk: ", $second as string, "\n";

Core\IO::remove("subject.txt");
Core\IO::remove("roster.txt");
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
body 0:
body 1: []
body 2: [one]
body 3: [one]
body 4: [one][][two]
first walk: 3
second walk: 3
